<h2 id="spares-heading">Spare Parts Strategy</h2>
<h3>Critical Spares (Stock On-Site)</h3>
<p>Parts that will shut down the conveyor (and therefore the line) when they fail, and that cannot be sourced locally within a few hours, should be stocked on-site. This typically includes: MDR rollers (one per unique type), servo motors and drives, specialty belting (one roll or set of modules), the PLC CPU and any specialty I/O modules, and any proprietary OEM components that cannot be delivered within your tolerable downtime. The investment in critical spares is insurance against downtime, calculate the cost of downtime per hour to justify the inventory value.</p>
<h3>Standard Spares (Stock Locally or Regionally)</h3>
<p>Parts that are standard industrial items local distributors can deliver quickly do not need to be stocked on-site in large quantities. This includes standard bearings, gravity rollers, motors, VFDs, sensors, contactors, and UHMW wear strips. Establish accounts with local industrial distributors and confirm they stock your common sizes. Keep 1–2 of each common sensor and a few spare bearings on-site for convenience, but do not over-invest in standard parts inventory.</p>
<h3>Documentation and Labeling</h3>
<p>Spare parts are only useful if your maintenance team can identify and install them quickly. Label every spare part with the conveyor manufacturer part number, the OEM component part number (bearing model, motor catalog number), the conveyor location where it is used, and the date it was placed in inventory. Maintain a spare parts list, either a spreadsheet or CMMS entry, that cross-references part numbers to conveyor locations and includes the supplier name and reorder information. When a <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/">manufacturer</a> discontinues a part, update the cross-reference with the replacement part number.</p>
