<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What are the most common conveyor parts that need replacement?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>The most frequently replaced conveyor parts are belts (fabric, modular plastic, wire mesh), rollers (gravity, powered, return), bearings (pillow block, flange mount, sealed), and wear strips (UHMW guides and bed plates). These components experience direct product contact, friction, and load stress. Motors, drives, sensors, and controls components fail less frequently but are more expensive and disruptive to replace. Actual replacement intervals depend on load, speed, hours, environment, and maintenance, so track failures on your own lines to set them.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Should I buy OEM or aftermarket conveyor parts?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>For standard components like bearings, motors, VFDs, and chain, buying the same catalog part from an industrial distributor instead of the conveyor builder is usually equivalent and often costs less. Bearing, motor, and VFD makers sell the same catalog parts regardless of which conveyor they go into. For proprietary components, custom frame sections, proprietary belt types, MDR rollers, and OEM-specific controllers, buy from the conveyor manufacturer or their authorized distributor.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How do I identify the correct replacement part for my conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Start with the conveyor's documentation: the parts manual, bill of materials, or maintenance manual should list part numbers for all wear components. If documentation is unavailable, identify the conveyor manufacturer and model from the nameplate, then contact the manufacturer's parts department. For standard components, measure the physical dimensions and match them to catalog specifications. Take photos of the part, including any stampings, labels, or markings. For belting, note the belt type, width, material, surface pattern, and pitch.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How often should conveyor belts be replaced?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Belt replacement intervals vary by belt type, product load, speed, environment, and maintenance quality. Modular plastic belts often last longer in service than flat belts on high-wear lines because damaged modules are replaced individually. Wire mesh belts in ovens and fryers wear faster as temperature and chemical exposure rise. Replace belts proactively when you observe tracking problems, visible surface wear, edge fraying, excessive stretch, or cracks and hardening from chemical or UV exposure.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">Where can I buy conveyor replacement parts?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Conveyor parts are available from the conveyor manufacturer (OEM) for proprietary and standard parts, industrial MRO distributors for standard components, belt manufacturers and belt fabricators for belting, power transmission and bearing distributors for bearings, chain, and gearmotors, and online marketplaces for common components. For critical or specialized parts, maintain a relationship with your conveyor manufacturer's parts department.</p>

</div>
</div>
